---
// `/gc-fitness/start` — the ONE link to put in a bio, a story or an ad.
//
// It renders nothing worth reading: it detects the OS and hands off to the matching store
// immediately, the way `hevy.go.link/download` does. `/gc-fitness/download` stays as the
// human-facing page (screenshots, copy, both badges) — this one is a turnstile.
//
// ## Why a client-side redirect and not a server one
//
// The site is Astro `output: 'static'` deployed to GitHub Pages (`.github/workflows/deploy.yml`).
// There is no server to sniff the user agent on, so the redirect has to run in the page. That
// is also what Adjust does for Hevy: `hevy.go.link/download` 302s to an `app.adjust.com`
// landing page which then sends the browser to `https://apps.apple.com/app/id…`.
//
// ## Two behaviours, not one — and #782 was right
//
// #782 established that on iOS EVERY `apps.apple.com` URL answers `301 → itms-appss://`, so
// there is no https page of the App Store to render inside a web view. Re-measured while
// building this page, with an Instagram user agent, for OUR app and for Hevy's:
//
//     GET https://apps.apple.com/ar/app/gc-fitness/id6771836254   → 301 itms-appss://…
//     GET https://apps.apple.com/app/id1458862350                 → 301 itms-appss://…
//
// Identical. The URL is never the variable. What differs is the HOST APP that receives the
// scheme, and it splits the world in two:
//
//   * WhatsApp, Safari, Android web views — the hand-off works. They get the plain automatic
//     redirect, which costs zero taps and is the best experience available.
//   * Instagram and the rest of the Meta family on iOS — the hand-off is refused, by EVERY
//     route. FOUR were tried on a real phone and all four failed:
//
//         1. automatic `location.replace(https store URL)`  → WHITE SCREEN
//         2. user TAP on the same https store URL           → nothing
//         3. `window.open('x-safari-https://…')`            → blank new tab
//         4. user TAP on `itms-apps://` directly            → nothing
//
//     The fourth was the last plausible one: the scheme as the INITIAL navigation of a user
//     gesture rather than as a network-layer redirect, which is a different branch of
//     WKWebView's `decidePolicyFor`. It failed too. Treat this as settled — there is no
//     programmatic route from Instagram's iOS web view to the App Store, and #782 was right
//     without qualification.
//
// For that second group the page therefore navigates NOWHERE and stops pretending it can.
// Its card carries the one route that has never stopped working — the host app's own ⋯ →
// "Abrir en el navegador" — as the headline, plus a copy-link button. Once the page reopens
// in Safari the ordinary redirect above finishes the job with no taps at all.
//
// The wrong turns are recorded in the body comments rather than deleted, because each was
// reasonable and each would be re-derived: that Instagram must accept the scheme (its chain
// ends on the same 301 as Hevy's), that a user gesture was the missing ingredient (Adjust's
// landing page only taps), that `x-safari-https` via `window.open` escapes (a project that
// MEASURES this per host app still lists it as working), and that the raw scheme on a tap
// was never actually tried in here (it wasn't — and it still doesn't work). None survived
// the phone.
//
// Two lessons worth keeping. The METHOD: on this problem, sources and inference both lose to
// one test on a real device. And the DESIGN RULE, which cost the most: a control that cannot
// work is worse than no control. It spends the one tap of attention we get and teaches the
// visitor the page is broken — which costs us the instructions sitting right below it.
import { APP_STORE_URL, PLAY_STORE_URL } from '../data/gcFitnessPublic';
import { defaultLang } from '../i18n/ui';
import type { Lang } from '../i18n/ui';

const lang = (Astro.currentLocale || defaultLang) as Lang;

const copy = {
  es: {
    title: 'Abriendo GC Fitness…',
    description: 'Te llevamos a la tienda de tu teléfono para descargar GC Fitness.',
    working: 'Abriendo la tienda…',
    // An INVITATION, not a diagnosis. For the Meta web views this card is the first thing on
    // screen and the tap on it is the mechanism, not a consolation prize — so the copy must
    // not open by apologizing for a failure the visitor never saw.
    tapTitle: 'Descargá GC Fitness',
    // Says what will actually happen. From Instagram the tap opens SAFARI, and Safari then
    // opens the store — promising "abrí la App Store" and delivering a browser switch reads
    // as a bug even when it is working exactly as designed.
    tapBody: 'Tocá el botón: abrimos Safari y de ahí la tienda.',
    tapCta: 'Abrir la App Store',
    tapCtaAndroid: 'Abrir Google Play',
    // The Meta-web-view variant. Action first, diagnosis second: the visitor wants the app,
    // not an explanation of WebKit. The ⋯ route is the ONLY one that has never stopped
    // working, so it gets the headline rather than a footnote.
    escapeTitle: 'Abrí esto en tu navegador',
    escapeBody:
      'Instagram no puede abrir la App Store. Tocá los ⋯ arriba a la derecha y elegí “Abrir en el navegador”: ahí la descarga arranca sola.',
    // Secondary, and deliberately phrased as a conditional: it only matters if the tap above
    // did nothing, and until then it is noise that competes with the real action.
    escapeHint:
      '¿No se abre? Tocá los ⋯ arriba a la derecha y elegí “Abrir en el navegador”.',
    appStore: 'Descargar en la App Store',
    playStore: 'Disponible en Google Play',
    copyLink: 'Copiar link',
    copied: '¡Link copiado!',
    seeMore: 'Ver la página completa',
  },
  en: {
    title: 'Opening GC Fitness…',
    description: "Taking you to your phone's store to download GC Fitness.",
    working: 'Opening the store…',
    tapTitle: 'Get GC Fitness',
    tapBody: 'Tap the button: we open Safari, and Safari opens the store.',
    tapCta: 'Open the App Store',
    tapCtaAndroid: 'Open Google Play',
    escapeTitle: 'Open this in your browser',
    escapeBody:
      'Instagram can’t open the App Store. Tap the ⋯ at the top right and choose “Open in browser” — the download starts by itself there.',
    escapeHint: 'Nothing happens? Tap the ⋯ at the top right and choose “Open in browser”.',
    appStore: 'Download on the App Store',
    playStore: 'Get it on Google Play',
    copyLink: 'Copy link',
    copied: 'Link copied!',
    seeMore: 'See the full page',
  },
} as const;

const t = copy[lang] ?? copy.es;
const downloadHref = lang === 'en' ? '/en/gc-fitness/download' : '/gc-fitness/download';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang={lang}>
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{t.title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={t.description} />
    <!-- A turnstile has nothing to index, and `/download` is the page that should rank. -->
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, follow" />
    <link rel="canonical" href={`https://goldencrowvs.com${downloadHref}`} />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png" />
    <!--
      Safari's Smart App Banner. Costs one tag and covers the case the redirect cannot: the
      app is ALREADY installed, and the banner offers "Open" instead of a store round trip.
      Ignored everywhere else, including inside web views.
    -->
    <meta name="apple-itunes-app" content="app-id=6771836254" />

    <!--
      In <head>, BEFORE the body paints. Every millisecond here is a millisecond of blank
      turnstile the visitor has to look at.
    -->
    <script is:inline define:vars={{ APP_STORE_URL, PLAY_STORE_URL, downloadHref }}>
      (() => {
        const ua = navigator.userAgent || '';

        // Twin of the detection in `GCFitnessDownloadPage.astro` — keep the two in step.
        // iPadOS 13+ reports "Macintosh"; `maxTouchPoints` is what gives it away (0 on a
        // desktop Mac, 5 on an iPad). Do NOT go back to `typeof document.ontouchend`: that
        // exists in touchless environments and called every Mac an iPad.
        const isIOS =
          /iPhone|iPad|iPod/i.test(ua) ||
          (/Macintosh/i.test(ua) && (navigator.maxTouchPoints || 0) > 1);
        const isAndroid = /Android/i.test(ua);

        // Stamped before the body paints so the card never flashes the wrong store's badge.
        document.documentElement.setAttribute(
          'data-gcf-os',
          isIOS ? 'ios' : isAndroid ? 'android' : 'desktop',
        );

        // Desktop has no store app to hand off to, so send it to the real page instead of
        // dropping someone on an Argentine storefront they cannot install from.
        if (!isIOS && !isAndroid) {
          window.location.replace(downloadHref);
          return;
        }

        // ── The Meta web views, and why they get NO redirect at all ─────────────────────
        //
        // MEASURED ON A REAL PHONE, which is the only way this was ever going to be
        // settled. Same URL, same iOS build, two host apps:
        //
        //   WhatsApp  → native "open in App Store?" prompt. Works.
        //   Instagram → WHITE SCREEN. Nothing else.
        //
        // So the difference is the HOST APP, not the platform and not the URL: WhatsApp's
        // web view implements `decidePolicyFor` + `UIApplication.open()` for the store
        // scheme, and Instagram's does not. #782 was right about Instagram all along; the
        // earlier reading of the Hevy link (that Instagram must hand the scheme off, since
        // Hevy's chain ends on the identical `301 → itms-appss://`) was wrong.
        //
        // The white screen is worse than a dead button, and worth understanding: a
        // top-level `location.replace()` makes the web view COMMIT to leaving — it tears
        // down the current document and paints nothing — and only then does the scheme
        // navigation fail. There is no "it didn't work, carry on" state to recover from,
        // and the safety net below cannot save it either: `pagehide` has already fired by
        // then, and the timer dies with the document.
        //
        // The only fix is therefore to NOT START the navigation here.
        //
        // ── And what these visitors get INSTEAD is the real mechanism, not a consolation ──
        //
        // Instagram blocks the PROGRAMMATIC navigation to the store scheme. It does NOT
        // block a navigation carrying a user gesture. The proof is the page we were asked
        // to copy: `hevy.go.link/download` 302s to an `app.adjust.com` landing page that
        // contains NO automatic redirect at all — no `location.replace`, no meta refresh —
        // just an app icon and an "Open app" button, both plain `<a href="https://…">`.
        // That is why their URL carries `enforce_landing_page=1`. The interstitial is not
        // laziness; it IS the mechanism, and the App Store prompt reported from Instagram
        // was a tap on it.
        //
        // ── And the tap cannot go to the store either. It has to go to SAFARI. ──────────
        //
        // MEASURED, round two: with the tap-to-install card live, NEITHER button opened the
        // App Store from Instagram. So the user gesture is not the missing ingredient —
        // Instagram refuses the store hand-off however it is triggered. The reading of
        // Adjust's page (no auto-redirect, therefore taps must work) was inference, and it
        // was wrong; what their landing page proves is only that they gave up on redirecting,
        // not that tapping succeeds.
        //
        // What the research says, and it is unanimous on the important half: from inside
        // Instagram's iOS web view there is NO way to reach the App Store. The only thing
        // that works is LEAVING first — once the page is in Safari, the ordinary redirect at
        // the top of this script does the rest. So the tap's job is the escape, not the store.
        //
        // Sources are split on how to escape, so this ships the chain rather than a bet:
        //   1. `window.open('x-safari-https://…')`. shalanah/inapp-debugger (a project that
        //      MEASURES these per host app; table current as of 2026-03-07) reports Instagram
        //      iOS as working "only via window.open" — a plain link or `location.href` with
        //      the same scheme is swallowed, which is what its issue #16 was about.
        //   2. `googlechromes://…`. The same table reports browser schemes working on
        //      Instagram iOS. Revealed only if step 1 leaves us on screen.
        //   3. The ⋯ → "Abrir en el navegador" instructions, which need nobody's cooperation.
        //
        // Ordered by how little the visitor has to do, and every step is one tap.

        //
        // Scoped to iOS on purpose. On Android the same web views render the Play Store
        // https page perfectly well, so redirecting there is still the better experience.
        const isMetaWebView = /Instagram|FBAN|FBAV|FB_IAB|FBIOS|Messenger|Threads/i.test(ua);
        if (isIOS && isMetaWebView) {
          document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-gcf-stuck', 'true');
          // Marks the card's actions as needing the ESCAPE chain rather than a plain store
          // link — see the body script. Set here, where the detection already lives, so the
          // two never disagree about which browser we are in.
          document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-gcf-escape', 'true');
          return;
        }

        // `https`, NOT `itms-apps://` / `market://`.
        //
        // In a real browser the scheme would save one 301, but inside a web view that drops
        // it the page dies with nothing rendered — whereas the https URL at least renders the
        // Play Store web page on Android. #782 has the measurements.
        const storeUrl = isIOS ? APP_STORE_URL : PLAY_STORE_URL;

        // `replace`, not `href`: the turnstile must not sit in history, or "back" from the
        // store lands the visitor right back on it and bounces them to the store again.
        window.location.replace(storeUrl);

        // ── Safety net for the web views we have NOT measured ───────────────────────────
        //
        // It cannot rescue the blank-screen case above — by the time a web view commits to
        // the navigation, this document is gone. What it does cover is the milder failure:
        // a host app that CANCELS the navigation without tearing the document down, leaving
        // us alive and visible with a spinner that will never finish.
        //
        // Only `visibilitychange` and `pagehide` count as proof we left. `blur` was tempting
        // as a third signal and is deliberately NOT here: it fires for reasons that have
        // nothing to do with leaving (focus moving, a keyboard dismissing), and a false
        // positive strands someone on that spinner. When in doubt, show the way out.
        let handedOff = false;
        const markHandedOff = () => {
          handedOff = true;
        };
        document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', () => {
          if (document.visibilityState === 'hidden') markHandedOff();
        });
        window.addEventListener('pagehide', markHandedOff);

        setTimeout(() => {
          if (handedOff || document.visibilityState === 'hidden') return;
          document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-gcf-stuck', 'true');
        }, 1600);
      })();
    </script>

    <noscript>
      <meta http-equiv="refresh" content={`0; url=${downloadHref}`} />
    </noscript>

    <style>
      :root {
        --gcf-amber: #e7b24a;
        --gcf-amber-deep: #a9761a;
        --gcf-ink: #1b1305;
        --gcf-muted: #6b5c3f;
      }

      * {
        box-sizing: border-box;
      }

      body {
        margin: 0;
        min-height: 100dvh;
        display: grid;
        place-items: center;
        padding: 24px;
        background: radial-gradient(circle at 50% 0%, #fffaee 0%, #f6efe0 100%);
        color: var(--gcf-ink);
        font-family:
          -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
        text-align: center;
      }

      .gcf-turnstile {
        display: grid;
        gap: 18px;
        justify-items: center;
        max-width: 26rem;
      }

      .gcf-logo {
        width: 84px;
        height: 84px;
        border-radius: 22px;
        box-shadow: 0 12px 32px rgba(27, 19, 5, 0.18);
      }

      .gcf-spinner {
        width: 28px;
        height: 28px;
        border-radius: 50%;
        border: 3px solid rgba(169, 118, 26, 0.25);
        border-top-color: var(--gcf-amber-deep);
        animation: gcf-spin 0.9s linear infinite;
      }

      @keyframes gcf-spin {
        to {
          transform: rotate(360deg);
        }
      }

      /* Someone who prefers reduced motion gets a static ring, not a strobe. */
      @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        .gcf-spinner {
          animation: none;
        }
      }

      .gcf-working {
        margin: 0;
        font-size: 1rem;
        font-weight: 600;
        color: var(--gcf-muted);
      }

      /* The escape hatch is hidden until the redirect proves it did not land. */
      .gcf-stuck {
        display: none;
        gap: 14px;
        justify-items: center;
      }

      :root[data-gcf-stuck='true'] .gcf-working,
      :root[data-gcf-stuck='true'] .gcf-spinner {
        display: none;
      }

      :root[data-gcf-stuck='true'] .gcf-stuck {
        display: grid;
      }

      .gcf-stuck h1 {
        margin: 0;
        font-size: 1.25rem;
        line-height: 1.3;
      }

      .gcf-stuck p {
        margin: 0;
        font-size: 0.95rem;
        line-height: 1.5;
        color: var(--gcf-muted);
      }

      /* The primary tap target. Sized like a native CTA because in a Meta web view it IS the
         mechanism — everything else on this card is a contingency. */
      .gcf-cta {
        display: inline-block;
        background: var(--gcf-amber-deep);
        color: #fff;
        font-weight: 700;
        font-size: 1.05rem;
        text-decoration: none;
        padding: 16px 32px;
        border-radius: 999px;
        box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(169, 118, 26, 0.32);
      }

      .gcf-badges {
        display: grid;
        gap: 10px;
        justify-items: center;
      }

      .gcf-badges img {
        height: 52px;
        width: auto;
      }

      /* Show only the badge for the OS we are on — offering an iPhone owner a Play badge is
         noise next to the one button that matters. `data-gcf-os` is stamped in <head>. */
      :root[data-gcf-os='ios'] .gcf-badges a:last-child,
      :root[data-gcf-os='android'] .gcf-badges a:first-child {
        display: none;
      }

      :root[data-gcf-os='android'] [data-gcf-cta-ios] {
        display: none;
      }

      :root[data-gcf-os='android'] [data-gcf-cta-android] {
        display: inline;
      }

      /* Variant switch. Default (no `data-gcf-escape`) shows the store arm; a Meta web view
         stamps the attribute in <head>, before paint, so neither arm ever flashes. */
      [data-gcf-when='escape'] {
        display: none;
      }

      :root[data-gcf-escape='true'] [data-gcf-when='store'] {
        display: none;
      }

      :root[data-gcf-escape='true'] [data-gcf-when='escape'] {
        display: block;
      }

      .gcf-arrow {
        position: fixed;
        top: 8px;
        right: 18px;
        font-size: 2.6rem;
        line-height: 1;
        color: var(--gcf-amber-deep);
        animation: gcf-nudge 1.4s ease-in-out infinite;
        pointer-events: none;
      }

      @keyframes gcf-nudge {
        0%,
        100% {
          transform: translate(0, 0);
        }
        50% {
          transform: translate(4px, -4px);
        }
      }

      @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        .gcf-arrow {
          animation: none;
        }
      }

      /* The instruction IS the primary action here, so it gets body-copy weight rather than
         the muted footnote sizing of `.gcf-escape`. */
      .gcf-escape-body {
        font-size: 1rem;
        line-height: 1.55;
        max-width: 24rem;
      }

      /* Promoted to the primary control when the store link cannot be one. */
      :root[data-gcf-escape='true'] .gcf-copy {
        background: var(--gcf-amber-deep);
        border-color: var(--gcf-amber-deep);
        color: #fff;
        padding: 14px 28px;
        font-size: 1rem;
      }

      .gcf-escape {
        font-size: 0.85rem;
        color: var(--gcf-muted);
        max-width: 22rem;
      }

      .gcf-copy {
        appearance: none;
        border: 1px solid rgba(169, 118, 26, 0.45);
        background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9);
        color: var(--gcf-ink);
        font: inherit;
        font-weight: 600;
        padding: 10px 22px;
        border-radius: 999px;
        cursor: pointer;
      }

      .gcf-more {
        font-size: 0.9rem;
        color: var(--gcf-amber-deep);
      }
    </style>
  </head>

  <body>
    <!--
      Points at the host app's ⋯ button, which lives at the top right of every one of these
      web views. The instruction is the highest-converting route we have and it competes with
      nothing on screen, so it gets an arrow rather than a sentence someone has to parse.
      Escape variant only — everywhere else there is no menu worth pointing at.
    -->
    <div class="gcf-arrow" data-gcf-when="escape" aria-hidden="true">↗</div>

    <main class="gcf-turnstile">
      <!--
        Plain image, not a link. It used to be tappable (copied from Adjust's landing page,
        where the icon IS the CTA) but in the branch that matters that tap cannot work, and
        an icon that silently does nothing is the same dead-tap tax as the button that was
        just removed. The store case has a real CTA below.
      -->
      <img class="gcf-logo" src="/gc-fitness/icon.png" alt="GC Fitness" width="256" height="256" />

      <div class="gcf-spinner" role="status" aria-label={t.working}></div>
      <p class="gcf-working">{t.working}</p>

      <div class="gcf-stuck">
        <!--
          Two headline/body variants, one slot. `data-gcf-escape` picks which — a Meta web
          view gets the ⋯ instructions AS the headline, because there the store is
          unreachable and an "open the store" promise would be a lie; every other stranded
          case gets the store CTA, where the link genuinely works.
        -->
        <h1 data-gcf-when="store">{t.tapTitle}</h1>
        <p data-gcf-when="store">{t.tapBody}</p>

        <h1 data-gcf-when="escape">{t.escapeTitle}</h1>

        <!--
          NO STORE BUTTON IN THIS BRANCH. FOUR ROUTES WERE TRIED AND ALL FOUR ARE DEAD:
            1. automatic `location.replace(https store URL)`  → WHITE SCREEN
            2. user TAP on the same https store URL           → nothing
            3. `window.open('x-safari-https://…')`            → blank new tab
            4. user TAP on `itms-apps://` directly            → nothing
          The fourth was the last untested one — the scheme as the INITIAL navigation of a
          gesture rather than as a network-layer redirect — and it failed too. There is no
          programmatic route from Instagram's iOS web view to the App Store. #782 was right
          without qualification.

          A button that does nothing is worse than no button: it consumes the one tap of
          attention we get and teaches the visitor the page is broken, which costs us the
          instructions right below it. So the instructions ARE the interface here.
        -->
        <p class="gcf-escape-body" data-gcf-when="escape">{t.escapeBody}</p>

        <!--
          PRIMARY action. An `<a>` and never a scripted navigation: the whole point of this
          branch is that the store hand-off must carry a user gesture.
        -->
        <a class="gcf-cta" href={APP_STORE_URL} data-gcf-store-link data-gcf-when="store" rel="noopener">
          <span data-gcf-cta-ios>{t.tapCta}</span>
          <span data-gcf-cta-android hidden>{t.tapCtaAndroid}</span>
        </a>

        <!--
          Store badges are hidden in the escape branch for the same reason: on iOS-Meta they
          are three more taps that cannot work. They stay for every OTHER stranded case,
          where the link genuinely opens the store.
        -->
        <div class="gcf-badges" data-gcf-when="store">
          <a href={APP_STORE_URL} data-gcf-store-link rel="noopener">
            <img src="/gc-fitness/app-store-badge.svg" alt={t.appStore} height="52" width="156" />
          </a>
          <a href={PLAY_STORE_URL} rel="noopener">
            <img src="/gc-fitness/google-play-badge.png" alt={t.playStore} height="52" width="175" />
          </a>
        </div>

        <!-- Only for the non-Meta stranded case; in a Meta web view this IS the headline. -->
        <p class="gcf-escape" data-gcf-when="store">{t.escapeHint}</p>

        <button class="gcf-copy" id="gcf-copy" type="button" data-copied={t.copied}>
          {t.copyLink}
        </button>

        <a class="gcf-more" href={downloadHref}>{t.seeMore}</a>
      </div>
    </main>

    <script is:inline define:vars={{ PLAY_STORE_URL }}>
      // On Android every store link on the card points at Play. The markup is authored once
      // with the App Store URL and rewritten here rather than duplicated per platform, so a
      // future URL change has exactly one place to be wrong.
      (() => {
        if (document.documentElement.getAttribute('data-gcf-os') !== 'android') return;
        document.querySelectorAll('[data-gcf-store-link]').forEach((link) => {
          link.setAttribute('href', PLAY_STORE_URL);
        });
      })();

      // ── Meta web views on iOS: NOTHING here navigates. Three attempts proved why. ─────
      //
      // Every programmatic route out of Instagram's iOS web view has now been tried ON A
      // REAL PHONE, and each failed in a way that was worse than doing nothing:
      //
      //   1. Automatic `location.replace(storeUrl)`  → WHITE SCREEN. The web view commits
      //      to leaving, tears down the document, then the `itms-appss://` navigation dies
      //      with nothing left to paint.
      //   2. A user-gesture TAP on the same store URL → nothing at all. So the gesture was
      //      never the missing ingredient; Instagram refuses the store hand-off by any route.
      //   3. `window.open('x-safari-https://…')`      → a BLANK NEW TAB, still inside
      //      Instagram. The scheme is dead there, and `window.open` makes its corpse visible
      //      as a tab the visitor now has to back out of. `googlechromes://` is not shipped
      //      either: it is the same class of bet, and losing it costs another dead tap.
      //
      // Attempt 3 was sourced from shalanah/inapp-debugger, whose table still lists the
      // Safari scheme as working on Instagram iOS "only via window.open". That measurement
      // did not hold here — which is exactly what its own issue #16 ("the Safari https link
      // doesn't work anymore") reported. Meta ships weekly and profits from keeping people
      // inside; any scheme-based escape is a bet on an undocumented behaviour of an adversary.
      //
      // So this branch navigates NOWHERE. The card's job is to make the ONE route that has
      // never stopped working — the host app's own ⋯ → "Abrir en el navegador" — as easy to
      // follow as a button would be. The store links keep their real `href` so a long-press
      // → "Copiar" still yields something useful, but no click handler hijacks them into a
      // dead end.
      //
      // If this is ever revisited: the failure to design around is a BLANK SCREEN, not a
      // dead button. A visitor who lands on white has no way back to the instructions.

      // Copy-the-link, for the web views where "Open in browser" is buried. Same shape as the
      // one on `/download`: `navigator.clipboard` is missing in plenty of web views, so the
      // off-screen textarea + `execCommand` fallback is not optional.
      (() => {
        const button = document.getElementById('gcf-copy');
        if (!button) return;

        const original = button.textContent;
        const copied = button.dataset.copied || original;

        button.addEventListener('click', async () => {
          // THIS page's URL, not `/download`.
          //
          // The instruction right above the button is "paste it into Safari", and in Safari
          // this page does exactly what it was built to do: hand off to the store with no
          // further taps. Copying `/download` instead would land them on a page where they
          // still have to find and press a badge — trading one tap for another for no
          // reason. It is also the right thing to hand to someone else.
          const link = window.location.href;

          try {
            await navigator.clipboard.writeText(link);
          } catch {
            const field = document.createElement('textarea');
            field.value = link;
            field.setAttribute('readonly', '');
            field.style.position = 'absolute';
            field.style.left = '-9999px';
            document.body.appendChild(field);
            field.select();
            try {
              document.execCommand('copy');
            } catch {
              /* Nothing left to try — leave the button label alone. */
              document.body.removeChild(field);
              return;
            }
            document.body.removeChild(field);
          }

          button.textContent = copied;
          setTimeout(() => {
            button.textContent = original;
          }, 2000);
        });
      })();
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
